      "text": "To determine the number of toothpicks in the 250th stage, we need to observe the pattern of toothpick usage. The first three stages of the pattern are as follows:\n\n- **Stage 1:**\n  - The initial stage consists of one complete square, which has 4 toothpicks.\n  - Toothpicks used in Stage 1: \\(4\\)\n\n- **Stage 2:**\n  - The second stage adds three new toothpicks for each side of the square.\n  - To create a new square, we add 4 new toothpicks (one additional toothpick for each side of the smaller square).\n  - Toothpicks used in Stage 2: \\(4 + 3 = 7\\)\n\n- **Stage 3:**\n  - The third stage adds three new toothpicks for each side of the new square.\n  - To create a new square, we add 4 new toothpicks (one additional toothpick for each side of the even smaller square).\n  - Toothpicks used in Stage 3: \\(7 + 3 = 10\\)\n\nFrom this pattern, we can see that the number of toothpicks used in each stage follows an arithmetic sequence. Specifically, the number of toothpicks in the \\(n\\)-th stage is given by:\n\n\\[\na_n = 4 + 3(n-1) = 3n + 1\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the number of toothpicks in the 250th stage, so we substitute \\(n = 250\\) into the formula:\n\n\\[\na_{250} = 3 \\cdot 250 + 1 = 750 + 1 = 751\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of toothpicks necessary to create the arrangement for the 250th stage is \\(\\boxed{751}\\).",
